          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes is primarily responsible for bo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes is primarily responsible for both resisting phagocytosis and triggering rheumatic fever through molecular mimicry of cardiac myosin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptolysin O</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Streptolysin O destroys red and white blood cells but does not mimic cardiac tissue. It serves as a serological marker — the ASO titre — for confirming past streptococcal infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>M protein</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>M protein serves a dual role: it inhibits phagocytosis by preventing opsonisation, and its structural similarity to cardiac myosin triggers cross-reactive antibodies that attack the heart in rheumatic fever.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyaluronidase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyaluronidase breaks down hyaluronic acid in connective tissue, helping the bacteria spread locally through tissues. It plays no part in molecular mimicry or phagocytosis resistance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptokinase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Streptokinase dissolves fibrin clots to facilitate bacterial spread. It is a tissue-spreading enzyme, not a factor involved in immune evasion or cardiac mimicry.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "M protein has a dual function: it resists phagocytosis AND mimics cardiac myosin. This molecular mimicry is the reason untreated streptococcal pharyngitis can trigger rheumatic fever 2–4 weeks later — and why prompt penicillin treatment is essential."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! M protein is both the anti-phagocytic shield and the molecular mimic of cardiac myosin — this dual role is exactly why prompt penicillin treatment of GAS pharyngitis prevents rheumatic fever."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
