          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 60-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with severe pneumonia and thick, blood-</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 60-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with severe pneumonia and thick, blood-tinged 'currant jelly' sputum with upper lobe involvement on chest X-ray. Which organism is most likely responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptococcus pneumoniae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>S. pneumoniae causes lobar pneumonia with rusty-coloured sputum and pleuritic chest pain. Currant jelly sputum in an alcoholic points to a different organism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Legionella pneumophila</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Legionella is acquired from contaminated water systems (cooling towers, air-conditioning units) and characteristically presents with hyponatraemia and confusion — not currant jelly sputum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Klebsiella pneumoniae</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Klebsiella pneumoniae causes Friedlander's pneumonia, classically seen in alcoholics and diabetics. Its hallmarks are thick blood-tinged 'currant jelly' sputum and a predilection for the upper lobes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall (invisible on Gram stain) and causes mild 'walking pneumonia' in young adults living in close quarters — not a severe pneumonia with currant jelly sputum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The combination of chronic alcoholism, currant jelly sputum, and upper lobe involvement is the classic triad for Klebsiella pneumoniae (Friedlander's pneumonia). Compare this with S. pneumoniae's rusty sputum and Legionella's hyponatraemia to keep the four organisms distinct."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Klebsiella pneumoniae causes Friedlander's pneumonia in alcoholics and diabetics — the currant jelly sputum and upper lobe involvement are its signature features, distinguishing it from S. pneumoniae's rusty sputum."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
