          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which mechanism of influenza viral evolution is responsible for causing pandemic</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which mechanism of influenza viral evolution is responsible for causing pandemics rather than seasonal outbreaks?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Antigenic drift through gradual point mutations</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antigenic drift involves small, cumulative mutations in haemagglutinin and neuraminidase genes. These minor changes cause seasonal outbreaks and necessitate annual vaccine updates, but the population retains partial immunity — not enough novelty for a pandemic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Antigenic shift through major genetic reassortment</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antigenic shift occurs when major reassortment of RNA segments produces a fundamentally new combination of surface proteins. The population has no pre-existing immunity to this novel strain, enabling pandemic-scale spread.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Recombination of single-stranded DNA segments</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Influenza is an RNA virus with a segmented genome — it does not use DNA recombination. Antigenic variation occurs through mutation (drift) or reassortment of RNA segments (shift).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Loss of the neuraminidase surface protein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Neuraminidase is essential for releasing new viral particles from host cells. Its loss would impair viral spread, not enhance it. Pandemics arise from gaining novel surface protein combinations, not losing them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the contrast: antigenic drift (small mutations) causes seasonal outbreaks requiring annual vaccine updates, while antigenic shift (major genetic reassortment) produces entirely new surface protein combinations that the population has never seen — causing pandemics."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Antigenic shift — major reassortment of RNA segments producing novel surface proteins — is what drives pandemics, because the population lacks pre-existing immunity to the new strain."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
