          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child presents with fever, sore throat, and a greyish-white pseudomembrane ove</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child presents with fever, sore throat, and a greyish-white pseudomembrane over the tonsils that bleeds on removal. After collecting a throat swab, which stain should you request first to identify the suspected causative organism?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gram stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gram stain screens for bacteria generally and would show bacillary morphology, but it cannot demonstrate the characteristic metachromatic granules needed to specifically identify Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ziehl-Neelsen stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ziehl-Neelsen stain identifies acid-fast bacilli such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It has no role in diagnosing pharyngeal infections with pseudomembranes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>KOH mount</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>KOH mount reveals fungal elements such as Candida pseudohyphae with budding yeast cells. A bleeding pseudomembrane points to diphtheria, not oral candidiasis — whose white plaques scrape off easily without bleeding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Albert stain</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Albert stain specifically identifies Corynebacterium diphtheriae by demonstrating blue-black metachromatic granules within yellowish-green bacilli. A pseudomembrane that bleeds on removal is the classic clinical clue for diphtheria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When a pharyngeal pseudomembrane bleeds on removal, suspect diphtheria. Albert stain is the specific stain for Corynebacterium diphtheriae, demonstrating blue-black metachromatic granules within yellowish-green bacilli. Follow up with Elek's test for toxigenicity confirmation."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Albert stain is the specific stain for C. diphtheriae — look for blue-black metachromatic granules within yellowish-green bacilli. The next step would be Elek's test to confirm toxin production."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
